The CRM conditioning choice interacts with $\varepsilon$ (Appendix~\ref{sec:magnitude_appendix}). obs\_only remains stable across all tested $\varepsilon$ (range 12.5--16.8\%), peaking at 0.2. perdof exhibits progressive collapse: ${\sim}$14\% at $\varepsilon{=}0.03$ $\to$ ${\sim}$10\% at 0.1 $\to$ 4.7\% at 0.2 $\to$ 0\% uncapped (${\approx}$1.42). scalar (8.5\%) falls below VLA-only. obs\_only is used throughout.

\paragraph{Per-DOF mechanism.}
The perdof collapse arises from estimating a $d{=}7$ per-DOF covariance from $K{=}10$ samples. The sample covariance has rank $\leq \min(K{-}1, d) = 7$, appearing full-rank but with effective rank much lower because consecutive action steps are highly correlated. At small $\varepsilon$, the bounded correction masks noisy per-DOF estimates; as $\varepsilon$ grows, the CRM amplifies these errors, causing compounding drift. obs\_only avoids this failure mode entirely: it conditions on the frozen observation embedding and a global $L_2$ uncertainty signal, sidestepping per-DOF covariance estimation. This explains why obs\_only is robust across all $\varepsilon$ while perdof is not---the limitation is not CRM architecture but sample efficiency relative to action-space dimensionality.

